The function under test is open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule states: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text." Reply 1 is the string "```json\n{\"a\":1}\n{\"a\":2}\n```\n{\"a\":3}". Reply 2 is the string "{\"a\":3}". The rule requires Reply 1 to score the object {"a":2}, because the code fence holds objects and the last one inside it is {"a":2}; it requires Reply 2 to score the object {"a":3}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {"a":3}. The check, however, returns {"a":3} for both replies. For Reply 1, the fenced candidate is the entire fence content '{"a":1}\n{"a":2}', which is not a single valid JSON object, so the check skips it; the top-level scan then finds {"a":3} as the last top-level object and returns it. For Reply 2, the top-level scan finds {"a":3} and returns it. I would be wrong if the check returned {"a":2} for Reply 1, or if the rule were read as scoring the last top-level object even when a fence holds multiple objects.